   "message": {
    "id": "b9fd0229-d49c-4b68-8f20-8147acbb98a4",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1788406222.051,
    "update_time": 1788406223.7225933,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "If you've become a California resident, CA law expects you to get a California driver's license within **10 days** of establishing residency *if you drive*. Owning a car doesn't matter. If you don't drive at all, you're not required to convert it, but your VA license will eventually expire and you can't renew it with a CA address.\n\nPractical reasons to switch anyway: a CA license or ID is handy as local proof of address, and REAL ID for flights. Make a DMV appointment online. For a valid out-of-state license you'll usually do a vision test and a written knowledge test, but no drive test.\n\nRules can change, so check dmv.ca.gov for the current requirements."
     ]
    },
